Since there is no menu and hotkeys in popups only seem to work when the popup is displayed, I had the idea of adding a button the the toolbar and assigning the hot key to it.

So I've got this working. By adding the following in `org.parosproxy.paros.extension.manualrequest.http.impl.ManualHttpRequestEditorDialog` `getManualSendPanel()`:

```
KeyStroke findKeyStroke = KeyStroke.getKeyStroke(KeyEvent.VK_F, Toolkit.getDefaultToolkit().getMenuShortcutKeyMask()); 
Action findAction = new AbstractAction("findAction") {  
	private static final long serialVersionUID = 8365172573382425660L;
	@Override
	public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {     
         FindDialog findDialog = new FindDialog();
         if (getFocusOwner() instanceof JTextComponent) {
        	 findDialog.setLastInvoker((JTextComponent) getFocusOwner());
        	 findDialog.setVisible(true);
         }
    }
}; 

JButton findButton = new JButton("Find");
findButton.getActionMap().put("findAction", findAction);
findButton.getInputMap(JComponent.WHEN_IN_FOCUSED_WINDOW).put(findKeyStroke, "findAction");
requestResponsePanel.addToolbarButton(findButton);
```

It seems a bit kludgey. There's also an issue around find dialogs being orphaned when the manual request window is closed. But it pops with the hotkey and works to actually find things as expected \o/. I guess the button itself also needs an associated action, not just mapped to the hotkey (so that clicking works) .... that wasn't my focus :grinning: The text on the button needs to be i18n but that's a minor detail, I guess it could be an icon instead (magnifying glass?)....

What do you guys think?

Edit: Guess there's further to go. I just noticed this works for requests but not response (different toolbar) :cry:

The selected/focused component can be retrieved when the action is executed similar to the example you posted earlier. For example:
```Java
@Override
public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
    Component focusOwner = KeyboardFocusManager.getCurrentKeyboardFocusManager().getFocusOwner();
    if (focusOwner instanceof JTextComponent) {
        FindDialog findDialog = new FindDialog(SwingUtilities.getWindowAncestor(HttpPanel.this));
        findDialog.setLastInvoker((JTextComponent) focusOwner);
        findDialog.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```
(This assumes that `FindDialog` was changed to be constructed with a `Window`.)
